WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.122 --> 00:00:01.198
Every January,

2
00:00:01.218 --> 00:00:03.301
Ohio State's staff gets about ten days to sort

3
00:00:03.321 --> 00:00:06.186
through thousands of names in the transfer portal.

4
00:00:06.206 --> 00:00:10.196
Most of that work still happens in hallways, film rooms, and spreadsheets.

5
00:00:10.216 --> 00:00:14.345
Here's how Buckeye Edge helps, and what it will, and won't, do.

6
00:00:15.109 --> 00:00:16.984
Year one is a decision aid, not a crystal ball.

7
00:00:17.004 --> 00:00:19.291
It doesn't pick players.

8
00:00:19.311 --> 00:00:23.922
It gathers what's public, organizes what your staff already says and grades,

9
00:00:23.942 --> 00:00:24.562
and puts it all in one place.

10
00:00:24.582 --> 00:00:27.784
So you can look at every quarterback in the portal,

11
00:00:27.804 --> 00:00:31.164
and every one who might enter, faster, using your own method.

12
00:00:31.184 --> 00:00:32.386
Coaches decide.

13
00:00:32.406 --> 00:00:34.015
The system keeps score.

14
00:00:34.866 --> 00:00:36.178
Here's the loop.

15
00:00:36.198 --> 00:00:38.820
Portal Watch flags players who look likely to enter,

16
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.137
using signals we tested on real rosters,

17
00:00:41.157 --> 00:00:43.940
like Group of Five players who outperform their level.

18
00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.012
Staff bots research them, and show their sources.

19
00:00:47.032 --> 00:00:49.712
Coaches talk to Edge in the hallway or the film room,

20
00:00:49.732 --> 00:00:52.324
on a phone, a tablet, or the big screen.

21
00:00:52.344 --> 00:00:55.025
Edge answers out loud, and builds the report: roster needs,

22
00:00:55.045 --> 00:00:59.294
the Monday brief, film-room prep, the selection board.

23
00:00:59.314 --> 00:01:01.925
Anything that would contact someone waits for approval.

24
00:01:02.646 --> 00:01:05.369
So why not predict outcomes right away?

25
00:01:05.389 --> 00:01:06.162
Because the data is noisy.

26
00:01:06.182 --> 00:01:10.098
Our methodology lead, Dr. Robert Rippee, points to four sources of variance.

27
00:01:10.118 --> 00:01:12.064
One: how people grade film.

28
00:01:12.084 --> 00:01:13.593
Two evaluators can watch the same ten

29
00:01:13.613 --> 00:01:16.294
quarterbacks and disagree on most of them.

30
00:01:16.314 --> 00:01:18.006
Two: program effects.

31
00:01:18.026 --> 00:01:20.469
A quarterback who dominated at a smaller

32
00:01:20.489 --> 00:01:22.586
program faces a different world in the Big Ten.

33
00:01:22.606 --> 00:01:27.178
Three: whether the measures themselves are reliable and valid.

34
00:01:27.198 --> 00:01:31.007
And four: outcomes, what actually happened to the players we rated.

35
00:01:31.873 --> 00:01:34.784
In plain terms, we're trying to explain the error.

36
00:01:34.804 --> 00:01:39.342
If we can explain about half of it, a prediction tool becomes worth building.

37
00:01:39.362 --> 00:01:42.803
Until then, a model would just carry the noise forward.

38
00:01:43.616 --> 00:01:46.020
So this year starts with listening.

39
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:49.923
Before December: a demo for the general manager and head coach,

40
00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:52.542
a session to define what success looks like,

41
00:01:52.562 --> 00:01:57.303
including three ideal players at each position, and the needs assessment.

42
00:01:57.323 --> 00:02:00.359
During the portal window, January fourth through the thirteenth,

43
00:02:00.379 --> 00:02:02.738
a small team observes, with permission,

44
00:02:02.758 --> 00:02:05.872
and records film sessions as coaches think out loud,

45
00:02:05.892 --> 00:02:08.121
like the Manning brothers' broadcast.

46
00:02:08.142 --> 00:02:10.594
Those transcripts become the data set.

47
00:02:10.614 --> 00:02:14.235
After the window, we compare speed and coverage against last year.

48
00:02:15.249 --> 00:02:19.235
Year two: write down Ohio State's own standard measures,

49
00:02:19.255 --> 00:02:21.238
expand to more skill positions,

50
00:02:21.258 --> 00:02:25.231
map program effects, and check the model against the 2024 and 2025 classes,

51
00:02:25.251 --> 00:02:27.059
where we already know the answers.

52
00:02:27.079 --> 00:02:32.374
Year three: computer-vision agents measure film the way your staff defined it.

53
00:02:32.394 --> 00:02:35.494
And, only if validation holds up,

54
00:02:35.514 --> 00:02:37.987
a predictor that estimates how likely a player

55
00:02:38.007 --> 00:02:40.986
is to succeed at Ohio State specifically.

56
00:02:41.006 --> 00:02:43.915
Beyond that, more positions as the data allows.

57
00:02:44.724 --> 00:02:49.223
None of this starts without approvals: a sponsor, a data-sharing agreement,

58
00:02:49.243 --> 00:02:52.633
consent to record, and a privacy and compliance review.

59
00:02:52.653 --> 00:02:55.800
Those come first, and dates stay open until they're signed.

60
00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:58.006
Open the How It Works tab to take the tour,

61
00:02:58.026 --> 00:03:01.538
read the questions coaches ask most, or just ask Edge.
